Z||000004||WisconsinGT||10-23-2000||12:43 PM||||My vote would be a hopped up 2.8 or 3.4. 3800's are costly and a *****  to work properly. If you do not want headaches, go the 2.8 route.||||reg||1
Z||000005||TheFiero64||10-23-2000||12:59 PM||||If money was no object a V8 with a supercharger.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif"> ||||reg||3
Z||000006||Tigger||10-23-2000||01:28 PM||||One that runs for sure...  j/k<P>No seriously, look for the Briggs & Stratton swap I'm going to do on my Fiero this winter.  Right now I'm looking into the best centrifigal clutch setup and gear drive sprocket ratios.  For the motor mount I'm going weld a bracket to the cradle and eliminate the need for halfshafts and go with a solid straight rear axle.  Of course I need to re-wire a few things and make a throttle cable brackets, but it should be a fairly easy swap.  Only problem I'm running into is I need to find a long enough pull start cord so that it sticks out the driver's side vent cover.  That way I can pull it, hop in, and go.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Okay seriously, I like the Northstar or V8 swap, but I also like the 4.3 or 3800SC swap.<P>------------------<BR>T-T-F-N, Tah Tah For Now!<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tigger.gif">           <BR>  <A HREF="mailto:RacingStripes@hotmail.com">RacingStripes@hotmail.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||william_thorniii||10-23-2000||03:04 PM||||Tigger,<P>Thank you... I can't believe the sheer #'s of pushrod guys out there.  For the style of driving that I do, an OHC engine suits itself best.  I seldomly if ever want power from the line, so massive amounts of torque is not necessary.  I use the Fiero to run through the twisties as fast as possible, where a higher revving engine is much better apt.<P>With a question as open as this, you are bound to start a HUGE discussion.  The simple matter of fact is that the use for the vehicle could completely change the answer for the "ultimate engine".  For my 40 mile drive to and from work, my Duke is almost the perfect engine.  <P>Thanks,<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||5
Z||000008||BBBAD GT||10-23-2000||04:08 PM||||I'm going to get alot of flack for this, but I'd love to put a Ford injected roller 302 back there with aftermarket heads/cam/intake, only because I'm so familiar with it.  But, since I've never seen it done or even know if its physically possible, the SHO V6 would be a sweet motor also (only seen pics of it).  <P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/BBBAD_GT_SIG.gif">    <P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD V8</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD GT</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0</A>||||reg||1
Z||000009||StuGood||10-23-2000||04:14 PM||||BBBADGT: Flak, flak, flak  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.  That's enough of a flak attack.  I'm done.<P>Wow! - Didn't know Briggs & Stratton made an OHC engine, WT III   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">?  <P>I change my vote - B & S, but the pull cord for starting has to be accessible by opening the center console storage box   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Now, <B>that's</B> class with a capital "K" !<BR><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 10-24-2000).]||||reg||10
