Z||000024||batboy||10-24-2000||08:09 PM||||WT3 has a good point. When my little 87 SE iron duke was running, it was perfect for my 42 mile commute. Of course, I love my 350 V8 Fiero, but it's a brute. If money was no object, I'd go with the 3800 series II supercharged swap. In my opinion, the 3800 is one of the best V6 engines being made right now. Too bad there isn't an handy dandy easy kit available. It is an expensive swap and fairly difficult unless you're a skilled shade tree mechanic. What I really want to try with my dead 87 SE, is to find a wrecked low mileage 1999 Gand Am GT to strip for parts, and be the first guy with a Ram Air 3400 OHV Fiero with a 4T45-E 4 speed auto tranny. Those babies put out 175 hp and 205 tq in stock form. Getting side tracked, but guess what? The 2001 Grand Am GT now has an optional Getrag 5 speed tranny available.||||reg||1
Z||000025||pollock||10-25-2000||03:21 PM||||BBBAD,<P>I gotta hi-five ya on the roller 5.0.  I'd step up to a 347 though.  <P>Ford small block in a fiero..  aaahhhh, yes...<P>Does the new Lincoln LS have a V8?  I'm trying to think of a Ford transaxle that could be used.||||reg||1
